PõXv/Ý©£c('Üý¦_îàšÎwŒûÀƒ5TÈÄ'Æ{"url":"/gcsl/language/values-and-bindings/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/language/values-and-bindings/","content":"Values and Bindings. GCSL programs compute values. A block can return one value or construct a record of named fields, so you can use configuration data inside ordinary expressions without a separate data format. Blocks and records # Newlines or semicolons separate items inside a file or { ... } block. A block without fields returns its final expression, or an explicit = expression result. A block with fields returns their record instead; it cannot also have an explicit result. The empty record {} represents an operation with no useful result. A field is a public part of the resulting record. A let binding is local to its scope. This block keeps the intermediate name private: Copylet release = \"0.1.0\" name = \"gcsl\" archive = \"{name}-{release}.tar.gz\" Top-level fields and named functions can refer to declarations written later. Inner blocks evaluate their ordinary fields in order. Private let bindings become available after their declaration. Updating a binding # := replaces the value associated with a local name. For an ordinary let, this is restricted to the block that declares it. Use let mut when an inner block of the same function needs to update the variable: Copylet mut total = 0 for cost in [3, 8, 5] { total := total + cost } total The variable keeps its declared or inferred type. Values themselves do not change: replacing a record field or list element constructs an updated value for the binding. Other bindings and previously created closures keep the values they already held. A closure captures values when it is created. It cannot update a mutable variable belonging to its enclosing function. Pass in a value and return an updated value when you need that communication. Names and arithmetic # Names can contain hyphens. Write spaces around subtraction so that left - right is an operation rather than a name. Integer arithmetic uses signed 64-bit values with checked overflow. Floating-point values use 64-bit precision. / produces a floating-point result; // and % are integer floor division and modulo. Integer literals adapt to an expected numeric type. A bound Int does not silently become a Float; use float(value) when that conversion is intended. Conditions require Bool, not a nonzero integer or a nonempty string. See gcsl(5) for syntax and operator rules. Durations and sizes # Duration and Size hold amounts with units. Write a duration as a number followed by units from largest to smallest: 30s, 500ms, 1h30m, 2d. The units are d, h, m, s, ms, us and ns, and a day is exactly 24 hours. Write a size with one unit: 512B, the decimal KB to PB, or the binary KiB to PiB, as in 2GiB. The last part of a literal can have a fraction if the result is a whole number of nanoseconds or bytes, so 1.5s and 0.5KiB are allowed but 1.5ns is not. Units are case-sensitive. Copylet timeout = 1m30s retry_after = timeout // 3 budget = 2 * timeout memory = 2GiB + 512MiB long = timeout > 1m message = \"giving up after {timeout}\" Add or subtract two values of the same kind, multiply one by an Int, and use // to divide by an Int or by another value of the same kind. Dividing by the same kind gives an Int. % gives the remainder of one value by another of the same kind. Values of the same kind can be compared. Durations and sizes never mix with each other, with Int or with Float, and an integer is never a duration, so write 0s rather than 0. / is not available, because the result would not be a whole number of the smallest unit. A value is shown as its simplest literal: retry_after above is 30s, budget is 3m, and memory is 2560MiB.","word_count":632,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / The Language","title":"Values and Bindings"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"blocks-and-records","text":"Blocks and records #","location":34},{"element":"h2","id":"updating-a-binding","text":"Updating a binding #","location":153},{"element":"h2","id":"names-and-arithmetic","text":"Names and arithmetic #","location":286},{"element":"h2","id":"durations-and-sizes","text":"Durations and sizes #","location":380}]}},"word_data":{"it":[{"position":56,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":73,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":107,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":178,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":217,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":258,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":261,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":269,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":618,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"simplest":[{"position":619,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"bound":[{"position":347,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"gcsl5":[{"position":374,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"30s":[{"position":404,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":624,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"64":[{"position":314,"weight":12,"original_word":null},{"position":322,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"later":[{"position":136,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"mut":[{"position":181,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":196,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"variabl":[{"position":194,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":215,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":266,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"kind":[{"position":519,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":540,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":545,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":561,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":566,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"public":[{"position":95,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"precis":[{"position":323,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"bool":[{"position":364,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"their":[{"position":70,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":140,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":151,"weight":24,"origina